  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 415 AM CDT Wed Jun 24 2026\n\nCurrently, upper level S/WV trough/closed mid level low over \nfar northeast North Dakota/northern Minnesota early this \nmorning. Wrap around boundary layer moisture rotating south \nacross the Northern Plains, contributing to low level cloud \ncover, scattered west and south central, with higher coverage \nnorth central and into my east attm. Models do show some \npotential for morning fog west, but for now will hold off on \nadding to the forecast and monitor. In addition to the low level\ncloud cover, scattered light showers persist near the low over \nnortheastern ND, including the Turtle Mountain region so will \nmaintain POPs there. \n\nWe remain in northwest flow aloft today as the aforementioned\nmid level low continues to move off to the east. Enough\nlingering boundary layer moisture and instability for another\nround of diurnally driven showers and a few thunderstorms during\nthe day north and into portions of the James River Valley. S/WV\nmid level ridge approaches and builds into the Northern Plains \nfor Thursday, resulting in mainly dry weather during the day.\nS/WV mid level impulse moves eastward across the Northern \nPlains Thu night, and will bring a chance for showers and a few \nthunderstorms from west to east Thu evening into Friday morning.\n\nLarge upper level low slides southeast along the western \nCanadian coast on Thursday and then just offshore of the \nPacific Northwest on Friday, with a long wave trough developing \nover the western CONUS. This results in southwest flow aloft \nfor our region, with lead waves bringing chances for showers and\nthunderstorms Friday aft and eve across the Dakotas, with a few\nstronger storms possible into southwestern ND. Over the \nweekend, southwest flow aloft increases, with models showing\nrather robust instability and favorable shear over the state,\nwith a continued barrage of embedded waves/frontal boundaries\nincreasing the chances for precipitation and setting the stage\nfor a few periods of strong to severe convection (SPC has our\narea still in the Day 4 outlook). NBM maintains fairly high POPs\nand QPF, focused on Saturday. In addition to the active weather\npattern, forecast temperatures this coming weekend will be\ncloser to normal for late June, with normal highs in the upper \n70s/low 80s and lows in the 50s.\n\nNBM and ensembles then favor a warming trend to above normal\ntemperatures next week towards the 4th of July period, with\nshower/storm chances more uncertain. Current analysis leans\ntowards more isolated/slight convective chances depending on\nseveral factors (such as convective inhibition, nocturnal LLJ,\nany embedded impulses moving through, etc..).",
